From 7f96e15ef504a4fa0796be6b06f2520787a31a36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 18:19:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(origin): one public address, in the running app and in
 every artefact

Why
---
The site told a crawler one address and a link preview another. The sitemap,
robots.txt, llms.txt and the per-route documents were generated from APP_URL,
while seventeen absolute URLs in client/index.html (canonical, both hreflang
tags, og:url, og:image, twitter:image and every @id in the structured data),
four in the PDF report and two build scripts carried the hosting address as a
literal. Worse, the running application agreed with the literals rather than
with the documents: shared/routes.ts read process.env.APP_URL, which does not
exist in a browser, so every client-side navigation rewrote the canonical link
back to the deployment address on a custom domain.

shared/routes.ts is now the origin register:

  * configuredOrigin() returns the operator's address or null - callers that
    must tell "configured" from "defaulted" ask it rather than comparing
    strings. siteOrigin() adds the fallback, siteHost() drops the scheme.
  * Two sources: APP_URL injected at BUILD time as __APP_ORIGIN__ by
    vite.config.ts (the browser has no process.env), then APP_URL from the
    environment for the server, the build scripts and the tests.
  * The fallback stays the deployment's own address, used only while APP_URL
    is unset, and is written down in exactly one place.

Everything else reads it:

  * client/index.html carries %SITE_ORIGIN% in its canonical link, hreflang,
    OpenGraph, Twitter and JSON-LD; a Vite plugin substitutes it in the build
    AND in dev, so what a developer sees is what ships.
  * report-pdf.ts derives the printed host and all three links, so a report
    forwarded to an owner or a bank does not send the reader to the hosting
    address.
  * analytics.ts resolves the Plausible domain as VITE_PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN, then
    the configured origin, then the current host: a preview deployment now
    reports to the registered site instead of a domain Plausible discards.
  * build-static.mjs and build-discovery.mjs call manifests.siteOrigin()
    instead of each keeping their own copy of the fallback.
  * client/public/robots.txt and client/public/sitemap.xml are deleted. Both
    are generated from the manifest and the origin; the checked-in copies
    listed four routes on the hosting address and were served whenever the
    generator had not run.

Invariants preserved
--------------------
  * shared/routes.ts stays the only route manifest, and is now also the only
    origin. No new dependency.
  * Nothing is invented: no domain is written down, and the fallback is
    unchanged. Setting APP_URL remains an operator action.
  * shared/ stays DOM-free; the build-time constant is read through a typeof
    guard, so the same module works in the browser, in Node and in the tests.
  * Health (0018) still reports APP_URL as a revenue blocker and refuses a
    localhost or plain-http value in production.
  * The operator record (0019) remains the only source of operator identity.

Tests added
-----------
  client/src/lib/origin.test.ts (11 cases): APP_URL wins; whitespace and
  trailing slashes are stripped; the fallback applies only while APP_URL is
  unset or blank; sitemap and robots.txt carry the configured origin;
  analyticsDomain() prefers the configured origin over the current host and
  falls back to the host when nothing is configured. Then the guard: the
  deployment address appears in no shipped file except the register, the
  static shell's own URLs all sit on the substituted token, the only absolute
  URLs left in it are schema.org and Stripe, vite.config.ts substitutes the
  token and defines the build constant, both build scripts call
  manifests.siteOrigin(), and no static robots.txt or sitemap.xml is shipped.

Not in this patch
-----------------
  * Buying or configuring a domain - an operator action. Set APP_URL and
    redeploy; `pnpm doctor` says so while it is missing.
  * Route and navigation work: the funnel's next paid step on every tool page,
    and the missing /pilot route in the manifest (0021).
  * README (0041).

